WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.236
WIL: Mike, Matt,
congratulations.

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.070 line:0%
You've made it to the next phase
in this operation, which means

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.472 line:0%
we're sending you back
to your home forges

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:12.478 line:0%
to recreate this iconic
weapon, the United

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.281 line:0%
States Air Force
non-commissioned officer's

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.182 line:7%
sword.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:17.150
 [sigh]

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:19.385
 You'll have four days
at your home forges

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.254
in which to complete
this challenge.

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.622
At the end of four days, you'll
return and present your swords

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:25.258
to our panel of expert judges.

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:26.993
And after they've put
them through the paces,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.795
they'll move one of you
one to our final Battle

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:33.165
of the Branches competition.

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.468
You also need to make a
scabbard for the sword.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:36.702
 Oh, man.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:37.970
 I'm just messing with you.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:38.938
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.607
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:41.374
 Good luck, brother.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.078 line:7%
MIKE: It's day one.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.247
It feels great to be back
here in my home forge.

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.116
For this blade, I'm going
to be using Damascus.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.518
And I'm going to try to put
in a twist just for fun.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.455
A lot of Air Force ties in
how I live my life day-to-day,

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:58.291
and this challenge
was no exception.

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:00.025
There's a lot of firsts
for me on this blade.

00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:01.895
I've never done
Damascus this long.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.495
I've never heat-treated
anything this long.

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.832
I've never made a
crossguard like this before.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.399
It's going to be a
bit stressful for me.

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:11.737
None of my welds popped, so
at least it's a solid billet.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:15.141
So we won't know for sure
until we're grinding into it,

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.410
but I'm fairly certain it
was a successful twist.

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.613
I don't think we
have any inclusions.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.049
I think it's going
to look pretty good.

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:25.184
So I've got 16 layers
now in this billet.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.685
I've got it drawn out enough.

00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:29.556
I can cut it into three sections
and stack it back on itself

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.390
for a total of 48 layers.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.226
This is going to be one of
the longest things I've ever

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:35.061
forged, so keeping
it straight might

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:36.529
be a little bit of a
challenge, especially

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:37.563
with the twist in it.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.131
That could cause
some things to warp.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.367
All in all, a solid
finish for day one.

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:43.736
[music playing]

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.305
MATT: Now that I'm one
of two [inaudible] left,

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:47.673
I definitely want
to be, you know,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.475
the one that shows
that the Air Force has

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:54.347
what it takes to win the "Forged
in Fire" branches competition.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:59.752
The plan for today is to draw
the 1084 round stock out.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:02.955
I'm just going to draw the line
on the floor to give me an idea

00:02:03.021 --> 00:02:04.991
of how far I've drawn it out.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.627
To start, the 1084
steel was 20 inches.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:10.063
I've got to draw it
out to about 35 inches

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.199
to get the crossguard, the
handle, and the pommel.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.935
I've still got to draw it
out quite a bit, get it down.

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:19.438 line:0%
This 27- to 28-inch
sword, it's going to be

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:22.141 line:7%
interesting keep it straight.

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:24.343 line:0%
It is at negative 18
outside right now,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.446 line:0%
so I'm ready to get inside
and turn this forge on.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:30.082
So yesterday, I got
the blade drawn out.

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:32.351
This morning, we're
going for the quench.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.521
Getting something this long
hot and keeping it straight

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:40.159
is going to be one of my
biggest challenges right now.

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:42.795
Now, I've got a warping issue.

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.497
I might be heat
treating it again.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:46.265
In the back of your mind,
you're always thinking,

00:02:46.331 --> 00:02:48.301
did the heat treat not go well?

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:52.071
Quenching two times, we'll have,
you know, bad grain structure.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.173
I have a ton of concerns.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:00.747 line:0%
Slight warp, but
at this point, I'm

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:03.983 line:7%
definitely not quenching again.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.652 line:0%
MIKE: It's the
morning of day four.

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:06.986 line:7%
I didn't sleep very well.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:08.320
I still need to
finish the pommel.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.655
I need to finish the crossguard.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.925
And I haven't even
started the handle yet.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.726
I can tell we're
almost through, though.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.462
Almost there.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.230
I can feel it.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.665
I definitely think
this is a blade

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:21.333
worthy of having me
stand at the Air Force

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:23.535
and go on to the final round.

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:25.771
It is an example of
how I problem-solve,

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.408
how I look for solutions,
and how I don't give up.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.211
All right.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.881 line:0%
That's as good as
we're going to get.

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:36.282 line:7%
MATT: Day four.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:38.417 line:0%
And today, I need to get
the crossguard fitted up,

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.486 line:0%
the handle shape
wrapped with wire,

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.755 line:0%
and then the pommel
assembled as well.

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:44.891
[inaudible]

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.626
Looking at the
original NCO sword,

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:48.827
I was trying to figure out
what the best way to make

00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:50.729
that wire-wrapped handle was.

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:51.530
Safety wire.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:52.999
We used that in the military.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.200
I thought that was
good to kind of throw

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:56.235
a little Air Force-ism into it.

00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:57.670
It works perfect.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.271
I'm definitely nervous that
I have only one day left.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:00.740
In the back of your mind,
you're always thinking,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.041
do I have what it takes
to move on to the Battle

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.209
of the Branches?

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.045
I mean, that's what
it's all about.

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.212
That's it.

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:10.182
May the best airman win.

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:11.417
DOUG: All right, airmen.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:12.818
Now, I'll take your
weapon and deliver

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.455
some thrusts and slashes
on this ballistics dummy.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:17.423
Mike, you're up first.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:18.190
You ready?
- Absolutely.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:18.925
Let's do it.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.814
[laughter]

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.385
 All right, Mike.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.054
It's a very light sword.

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:50.189
Because of that, you
can move with it.

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:53.459
Now, your tip allows
for a nice thrust.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:55.160
With your edge, it slices.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.163
I can feel every cut in there.

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:00.165
But more importantly,
it will kill.

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:01.234
- Thank you.
- All right, Matt.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:01.967
Your turn.

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:02.835
You ready, sir?

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:03.569
 Yes, sir.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:23.089
 [sigh]

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:25.458
[chuckling]

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:27.192
 All right, Matt.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:28.694
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.530
The edge that you have
here is very sharp.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.666
The point punctures nicely.

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:37.637
And most importantly,
sir, it will kill.

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:38.237
 Awesome.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.004
Thank you.

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:39.972
 All right.

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:41.741
Next up, the strength test.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:45.044
For that, we have Ben.

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:46.412
 Hello, bladesmiths.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:47.646
To test the strength and
durability of your swords

00:05:47.746 --> 00:05:49.115
as well as their
overall construction,

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:52.685
I'll be bashing them
into this propeller.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.119
Mike, you're up first.
You ready?

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:55.387
 No.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:57.389
 [laughter] We're
going to do it anyway.

00:05:57.456 --> 00:06:00.059
 All right.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:03.029
[pounding]

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:04.897
 [sigh]

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:08.634
[pounding]

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.644
[clattering]

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:21.647
 Good job.

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:22.815
 Oh, my gosh.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.651
 Well, Mike, your
blade help up great.

00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:28.153
The only issue I have with
it is that I would really

00:06:28.253 --> 00:06:29.588
like this pommel to be bigger.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:32.057
If the pommel were bigger,
it would give me a stop.

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.193
But it's got a nice weight.

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:35.995
It's still sharp
as when it started.

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:36.963
Really well done.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:38.164
 Thank you so much.

00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:39.331
 Matt, you're up.
What are you thinking?

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:40.032
 I'm ready.

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:46.472
[pounding]

00:07:00.285 --> 00:07:01.220
[clattering]

00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:03.589
 That's badass.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:07.026
 Well, Matt, your
blade held up great.

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:08.293
It's still sharp.

00:07:08.393 --> 00:07:09.896
This is a much heavier sword.

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:14.066
But the biggest issue I have
is with the crossguard in back.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.368
Your crossguard is loose.

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:17.503
It wiggles.

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.906
And also, your
wire wrap is loose.

00:07:20.006 --> 00:07:22.474
But it's all in one piece.

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:24.010
It did a heck of a
thing on that propeller.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:24.810
Well done.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:25.610
 Thank you.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:26.679
BEN: But we're not finished yet.

00:07:26.745 --> 00:07:27.746
Next up is the sharpness test.

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:28.714
And for that, I
give you to Doug.

00:07:33.019 --> 00:07:34.086
 All right, airmen.

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:35.721
To test the edge of your
blade, I'm going to be

00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:38.657
cutting across these harnesses.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:41.126
I'm looking for clean cuts.

00:07:41.193 --> 00:07:41.994
Mike, you're up first.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:42.728
You ready for this?

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:43.395
 Let's do it.

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:44.130
 Let's do this.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:02.748
- Good job.
- Right through it.

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:03.616
 That was good.

00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:04.216
Sharp.

00:08:04.316 --> 00:08:05.818
 All right, Mike.

00:08:05.884 --> 00:08:08.887
Light in flight and sharp enough
to cut through the harness.

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:09.421
It will cut.

00:08:09.522 --> 00:08:10.456
 Thank you, sir.

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:11.290
 Good job.

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:12.691
 Thanks, man.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:14.093
 All right, Matt.
Your turn, sir.

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:14.894
You ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:26.105
Damn.

00:08:29.408 --> 00:08:30.476
DOUG: All right, Matt.

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:31.644
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:08:31.744 --> 00:08:33.178
It's kind of heavy.

00:08:33.278 --> 00:08:36.915
It's so forward in its weight
that on this slash, using

00:08:36.982 --> 00:08:39.484
the last six inches,
it just scratched

00:08:39.551 --> 00:08:41.053
the back of the harness.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.089
But on the backhand swing, where
it's going all the way through,

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:44.590
it cut the harness.

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:46.358
But your weapon,
sir, it will cut.

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:47.092
 Thanks.

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:47.826
 Good job, man.

00:08:47.893 --> 00:08:49.127
 Thanks.

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:54.500
 Mike, Matt, the bladesmith
moving forward is Mike.

00:08:54.567 --> 00:08:56.168
Congratulations.

00:08:56.235 --> 00:08:57.837
 Thank you.

00:08:57.904 --> 00:08:59.971
 Matt, first of all,
thank you for your service.

00:09:00.038 --> 00:09:02.208
You have a beautiful
sword that did quite well.

00:09:02.308 --> 00:09:03.976
But we based our
decision on the fact

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:06.679
that your guard came loose,
the weight of your sword,

00:09:06.745 --> 00:09:09.481
and the wire wrapping
is also loose.

00:09:09.548 --> 00:09:11.350
That's the reason
we're sending you home.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:12.551
 Yes, sir.

00:09:12.651 --> 00:09:14.686
 Matt, at this time,
I have to ask you

00:09:14.786 --> 00:09:17.356
to please surrender your blade.

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:18.723
MATT (VOICEOVER): I feel
good no matter what.

00:09:18.823 --> 00:09:20.392
I mean, I'm glad I made it here.

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:21.226
DOUG: Good job.

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:22.261
 Thank you.

00:09:22.327 --> 00:09:24.863
I'm proud of myself for sure.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.532
Mike's going to represent
the Air Force well.

00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:28.633
He's got a lot of people
rooting for him, me, as well.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.703
And he's a good
bladesmith, so good luck.

00:09:34.973 --> 00:09:38.010
 Mike, they said you're only
as good as your competition,

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:40.512
and you overcame a
fantastic competitor

00:09:40.579 --> 00:09:41.947
to move forward in
this competition

00:09:42.014 --> 00:09:45.418
to represent the entire
branch of the Air Force

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:47.386
at our Battle of the
Branches competition.

00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:48.220
How do you feel?

00:09:48.286 --> 00:09:49.488
 It feels amazing.

00:09:49.588 --> 00:09:51.790
 Please present your
blade to the judges.

00:09:51.857 --> 00:09:53.092
MIKE (VOICEOVER):
I can't believe

00:09:53.192 --> 00:09:54.527
I'm the Air Force champion.

00:09:54.627 --> 00:09:56.295
I'm looking forward to
showing the rest of the DOD

00:09:56.362 --> 00:09:57.596
what we've got.

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:59.030
There's a lot of added
pressure moving forward.

00:09:59.097 --> 00:10:00.666
Now, I'm competing against
the Army, the Navy,

00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:02.267
and the Marine Corps, and
we've got a little bit

00:10:02.367 --> 00:10:03.736
of a rivalry going on.

00:10:03.802 --> 00:10:06.739
So you better bring it,
because you know I'm going to.

